<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Ménière disease of the left ear, definite (H81.02)</content>. The presenting attacks of vertigo with left-ear tinnitus and muffled hearing now have their diagnosis. Dr. Okafor-Lindqvist finds definite Ménière disease of the left ear by the AAO-HNS 1521 and Bárány criteria: more than two spontaneous attacks of spinning vertigo lasting about one to six hours, low-frequency sensorineural hearing loss of 40 dB at 250 and 500 Hz on today&apos;s audiogram in the affected ear, and fluctuating tinnitus and fullness in that ear. Vestibular migraine was considered, because he has headaches, but no attack has come with migraine features, so its criteria are not met; the headaches will be recorded in the diary. The examination between attacks is normal, without signs of stroke. [S]<list><item>Sodium 1,500 to 2,300 mg a day; caffeine down to one cup a day; alcohol limited.</item><item>Hydrochlorothiazide 25 mg each morning; sodium and potassium in two to four weeks.</item><item>Meclizine only for severe attacks.</item><item>MRI of the internal auditory canals with gadolinium.</item><item>No betahistine.</item><item>Keep the attack and headache diary.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Major depressive disorder, recurrent, moderate (F33.1)</content>. The presenting low mood now has its diagnosis. On a structured diagnostic interview he has had depressed mood, loss of interest, broken sleep, fatigue, guilt and poor concentration for about two months, with a clear earlier episode in his twenties: major depressive disorder, recurrent, moderate, by DSM-5-TR, with anxious distress about the attacks. No past hypomania or mania, and the Mood Disorder Questionnaire is negative, so an antidepressant is safe to start. PHQ-9 14. The depression is not explained by the vertigo alone, though the attacks feed it. After discussing therapy alone, medicine alone, or both, he chose both. [S]<list><item>Weekly cognitive behavioral therapy with behavioral activation, adapted to his faith and the fear of attacks.</item><item>Sertraline 25 mg each morning for a week, then 50 mg.</item><item>Safety plan reviewed and unchanged; 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline and the clinic number confirmed.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Vertigo attack diary: number, duration, hearing, tinnitus, fullness, headache</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>One three-hour attack since intake; two headaches without vertigo; Dizziness Handicap Inventory 52 (moderate) (Dizziness Handicap Inventory in the notes; no LOINC code used)</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Audiometry (pure-tone and speech) with tympanometry</td><td>At diagnosis; repeat at about three months and with any change in hearing</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Left low-frequency sensorineural loss of 40 dB at 250 and 500 Hz; word recognition 88 percent left, 100 percent right; normal tympanograms</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>MRI of the internal auditory canals with gadolinium</td><td>Once, to exclude a retrocochlear lesion with asymmetric hearing loss</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Ordered today</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Vestibular function testing and electrocochleography</td><td>Not routinely for diagnosis</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>The guideline advises against routine testing to diagnose Ménière disease; the history and audiogram are enough</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Vestibular suppressant use</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>One meclizine dose in two weeks; limited to severe attacks</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Sodium, caffeine and alcohol limits</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Education given today</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Otology consultation</td><td>Once, at diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen by Dr. Okafor-Lindqvist today</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PHQ-9</td><td>At least monthly during acute treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>14, from 15 at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Mood Disorder Questionnaire at the initial depression workup</td><td>Once at the initial workup; repeat if hypomanic features emerge</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Negative; no past hypomania or mania</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide risk reassessment (C-SSRS Since Last Visit and PHQ-9 item 9)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No suicidal ideation, intent or plan; PHQ-9 item 9 scored 0. Safety plan reviewed</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>SSRI tolerability check two weeks after starting</td><td>2 weeks after starting and after each dose change</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Due in two weeks; with the sodium check on the thiazide</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Sodium, potassium, creatinine and calcium on a thiazide with an SSRI</td><td>Two to four weeks after starting, then every three to six months</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Sodium and potassium in two to four weeks</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Fewer and milder vertigo attacks: no more than one disabling attack a month by three months, and able to preach without fear of an attack. In progress; target 1527-10-27 [S]</item><item>Depression response (PHQ-9 down by at least 50 percent) by three months. In progress; target 1527-10-27 [S]</item><item>Sodium intake below 2,300 mg a day. In progress; target 1528-01-31 [S]</item><item>Urine volume of at least 2.5 liters a day. In progress; target 1528-01-31 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Clinical Practice Guideline: Ménière&apos;s Disease (2020); Diagnostic criteria for Menière&apos;s disease (2015); Vestibular migraine: diagnostic criteria (update) (2022); Efficacy and safety of betahistine treatment in patients with Meniere&apos;s disease: primary results of a long term, multicentre, double blind, randomised, placebo controlled, dose defining trial (BEMED trial) (2016); Clinical Practice Guideline for the Treatment of Depression Across Three Age Cohorts (2019); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monograph (2026); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Blood test in two to four weeks. Review in six weeks. Next scheduled visit 1527-09-08: periodic adult preventive visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
